<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.6.2: Analyzing Graphs of Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00942</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.6.2: Analyzing Graphs of Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0b1eab88-34fe-422e-ba27-f246ad6e2ef0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>A toy rocket and a drone were launched at the same time.</para>
<para>Here are the graphs that represent the heights of two objects as a function of time since they were launched.</para>
<para>Height is measured in meters above the ground, and time is measured in seconds since launch.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="A GRAPH OF TWO FUNCTIONS, EACH REPRESENTING THE HEIGHT IN METERS OF AN OBJECT AFTER TIME IN SECONDS HAS PASSED. THE HEIGHT OF THE TOY ROCKET IS MODELED BY AN UPSIDE-DOWN PARABOLA WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 25 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF 5, WITH A MAXIMUM AT THE POINT (2, 45). THE HEIGHT OF THE DRONE IS MODELED BY A FUNCTION THAT INCREASES LINEARLY FROM X = 0 TO X = 2, UP TO THE POINT (2, 20), REMAINS CONSTANT FROM X = 2 TO X = 5, AND THEN DECREASES LINEARLY FROM X = 5 TO X = 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/381549c4047fb4e339394723978be30242415483"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Analyze the graph of the toy rocket and describe as precisely as you can what was happening. Your description should be complete and precise enough that someone who is not looking at the graph could visualize how the object was behaving. Include where the toy rocket starts and when it stops.
        Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>The toy rocket was launched from a height of 25 meters above ground. It went up quickly, but it slowed down and then stopped getting higher when it reached 45 meters, which was 2 seconds after launch. At that point, it started to come back down. It hit the ground 5 seconds after it was launched.
    </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Analyze the graph of the drone and describe as precisely as you can what was happening. Your description should be complete and precise enough that someone who is not looking at the graph could visualize how the object was behaving. Include where the drone starts and when it stops.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>The drone took off from the ground. It went up at a constant speed for 2 seconds, up to a height of 20 meters. Then, it hovered at that height for 3 seconds. Afterward, it started descending at a constant speed for 2 seconds. It landed back on the ground 7 seconds after launch.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>At what point were the drone and the toy rocket at the same height?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>A little after 4 seconds, the drone and the toy rocket were at the same height (20 meters).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Which parts or features of the graphs show important information about each object’s movement? List the features or mark them on the graphs.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <list>
      <item>the points where the graphs begin (when \(t\) is 0)</item>
      <item>the points where the graphs cross the horizontal axis</item>
      <item>the point where the graphs change direction</item>
      <item>the shape of each graph (curved or straight)</item>
      <item>the intervals when the graphs behave certain ways</item>
      <item>the parts highlighted in the graph</item>
    </list>
    <figure><media alt="Two nonlinear functions. time in seconds and height in meters."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7560ec4450ed8d216a5e4c60ddd2b3e9a325465a"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
